# The QML modules the shell imports, matching the list the interface audit
# installs on CI - the application is known to run with exactly these.
# Declared as Recommends rather than Depends on purpose: apt installs those
# by default, so a normal install gets a working application, while a module
# whose package name moved between Debian releases warns instead of making
# the package uninstallable, which is the failure mode that matters for a
# package installed by hand off a release page. Without them at all the shell
# reports "module QtQuick.Controls is not installed" over a black window.
Recommends: qml6-module-qtquick,
            qml6-module-qtquick-controls,
            qml6-module-qtquick-dialogs,
            qml6-module-qtquick-layouts,
            qml6-module-qtquick-templates,
            qml6-module-qtquick-window,
            qml6-module-qtqml-workerscript,
            qml6-module-qtwebchannel,
            qml6-module-qtwebengine,
            yt-dlp
Description: native YouTube Music desktop client
 Melyar is a lightweight YouTube Music client for Linux. A Rust core handles
 the InnerTube API, the stream cipher, mpv playback, the local library and the
 Linux desktop integration, a Qt 6 shell hosts it, and a React interface draws
 it.
 .
 It speaks to YouTube Music directly, so it needs no account of its own and no
 cloud service. Playback runs through libmpv, the library is kept in a local
 SQLite database, and the interface speaks thirteen languages.
 .
 The client integrates with the desktop rather than sitting on top of it:
 MPRIS for media keys, a Unix socket for Waybar and Polybar, global shortcuts
 and a desktop entry.
 .
 yt-dlp is optional. It is used when it is installed, because it handles some
 of the harder parts of the protocol, but the application resolves streams
 without it as well.
